    "narrative": "For today\u2019s slate, **James Wood** (76.28) and **Mickey Moniak** (75.98) headline our offense, both benefitting from favorable matchups against Miles Mikolas and Michael Lorenzen. **James Wood** draws an elite 0.428 xwOBA projection and benefits from wind blowing out, while **Mickey Moniak** sees a massive 14% boost from a favorable park environment. Both players remain high-priority starts because they offer elite stolen base potential against pitchers with slower deliveries, a critical factor for us in this league. **Vinnie Pasquantino** (69.99) and **Isaac Paredes** (68.21) also represent strong plays, as both benefit from dynamic platoon advantages against right-handed pitching, providing the power profile necessary to anchor our lineup despite lingering concerns over officially posted batting orders.\n\nThe most difficult roster decision today was leaving **Austin Riley** (62.89) on the bench. While his score is respectable, he narrowly missed the cut due to the superior platoon and park upside provided by **Kyle Karros**, whose 63.20 score was inflated by the favorable conditions at the park and his spot in the lineup. We are closely monitoring the 41% rain risk affecting **Seiya Suzuki**, whose score of 62.95 is dampened by a \"wind in\" factor that hinders his power ceiling. While **Corbin Carroll** (64.96) is active despite a tougher matchup against Jack Perkins, his elite speed keeps him locked into our starting rotation as we continue to prioritize stolen bases across our lineup. We remain cautious with **Gabriel Moreno** and **Travis Bazzana**, who hover in the marginal range (51-53) and are entirely dependent on their projected spots in the batting order to sustain their daily efficiency.",
